<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  plot-registryd, kept alive by launchd — macOS.

  Replace the four placeholders before installing:
    __LABEL__       the launchd label (`plot-fleetctl.sh` fills PLOT_FLEET_LABEL,
                    default com.plot-pm.registryd)
    __REPO_ROOT__   absolute path to the repository this daemon supervises
    __NODE__        absolute path to node 24 (`which node` under `nvm use`)
    __REGISTRYD__   absolute path to plot-registryd.mjs

  Install: skills/plot/units/README.md
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>__LABEL__</string>

  <!-- ONE DAEMON PER REPOSITORY. The default label carries no repository name,
       so a second checkout sets PLOT_FLEET_LABEL before `plot-fleetctl.sh
       --start`, which fills it here and names the file after it — see the
       README. launchd keys a job by this Label, not by the filename: two files
       sharing one Label are ONE job, and the second bootstrap fails. -->

  <!-- `--start-agents` IS WHAT MAKES THIS A SUPERVISOR. The flag defaults off so
       that `/plot-fleet --once` can run against a live estate for free — a tick
       without it decides every hand-over and performs none. The long-running
       unit is the one place where that is nobody's intent. Measured 2026-09-07:
       three consecutive ticks reported `handed=2` while both free agents'
       manifests read `branch: ""` and had been quiet 3,067 seconds. -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>__NODE__</string>
    <string>__REGISTRYD__</string>
    <string>--start-agents</string>
  </array>

  <!-- THE DAEMON READS THE REPOSITORY IT IS STARTED IN. `registryd-main.ts`
       takes `PLOT_REPO_ROOT` and falls back to `process.cwd()`; both are set
       here so neither the fallback nor a launchd working-directory default
       decides which estate is supervised. -->
  <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
  <string>__REPO_ROOT__</string>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PLOT_REPO_ROOT</key>
    <string>__REPO_ROOT__</string>
    <!-- git, gh, node and the agent harness (claude, or the name in
         PLOT_HARNESS) must be findable: launchd gives a job a minimal PATH and
         none of the four is on it. The first entry is the directory the fleet
         start resolved the harness in with `command -v`, so a worker runs the
         binary the operator's shell runs. It comes first for EVERY binary in
         that directory, not only the harness; on a typical install that is
         ~/.local/bin. Add homebrew's prefix for your arch. -->
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>__HARNESS_DIR__:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
  </dict>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- KEEPALIVE IS THE WHOLE POINT, and it is unconditional. The daemon exits
       only when killed or when the machine stops it; every recoverable failure
       is now a tick that reports and continues, so an exit means the process
       itself is gone and restarting it is always right. -->
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- A CRASH LOOP IS THROTTLED, not fought. launchd's floor is 10 s; this
       raises it to the tick interval, so a daemon that cannot start does not
       spend a core on the attempt. -->
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>60</integer>

  <!-- Two streams, two files. The tick lines go to stdout and an INCOMPLETE
       tick goes to stderr, so `tail -f` on the error log shows exactly the
       ticks that could not be taken. -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>__REPO_ROOT__/.plot/logs/registryd.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>__REPO_ROOT__/.plot/logs/registryd.err</string>

  <!-- PRIORITY AND EVICTION ELIGIBILITY ARE TWO THINGS, and `Background` bought
       both. The daemon is idle 94% of a 60 s interval and shares a machine with
       the workers it supervises, so it must never be what makes a worker slow —
       that reasoning is right, and it argues for scheduling priority alone.

       Measured 2026-09-07: the job was removed SIX times in one session.
       `runs = 1`, `last exit code = (never exited)`, `registryd.err` 0 bytes and
       no launchd log line each time — removed by the system, not crashed, and
       it never came back on its own. Lifetimes ran 48 s to 17 min.

       WHAT TRIGGERS IT IS NOT KNOWN, and load is not it: one incarnation died
       at load 4.30 and another served at 30.70. Five explanations were tested
       and disproved — load, memory pressure, `AbandonProcessGroup` (with a
       control unit), `--once`, and `--start` racing a loaded job.

       THE EVIDENCE FOR THIS KEY IS THE COMPARISON, NOT THE TRIGGER. `node
       --watch board-server.mjs` ran 2 days 12 hours unbroken beside it on the
       same machine: two long-lived Node processes in one repo, and only the one
       declaring `Background` is taken. That is the whole argument, and it does
       not depend on knowing when.

       THE SYSTEMD UNIT NEEDS NO SUCH CHANGE, and that is the argument rather
       than an inconsistency: `Nice=10` and `IOSchedulingClass=idle` are
       priority alone and evict nothing. The two platforms disagreed only in the
       field that matters.

       `Adaptive` keeps the job schedulable without the throughput class's
       eviction; `Nice` carries the politeness the reasoning above asks for. -->
  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Adaptive</string>
  <key>Nice</key>
  <integer>10</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
